-- title:
--   Germ ideals: chart independence, extensionality, support dichotomy
-- statement:
--   Let $X$ be a scheme. For a point $x \in X$, an affine open $U$ of $X$ containing $x$ and an ideal sheaf datum $I$ on $X$ (a family assigning to each affine open $U$ an ideal $I.ideal\,U$ of $\Gamma(X, U)$, compatible under restriction to affine opens), write $\mathrm{Ideal.map}$ of the germ homomorphism $\Gamma(X,U) \to \mathcal{O}_{X,x}$ applied to $I.ideal\,U$ for the ideal of the stalk generated by the germs of the sections in $I.ideal\,U$. The theorem asserts the conjunction of three statements. First, for every $I$, every $x$ and all affine opens $V, W$ containing $x$, the two ideals of $\mathcal{O}_{X,x}$ so obtained from $V$ and from $W$ coincide; that is, the germ ideal at $x$ does not depend on the chosen affine chart. Second, for all ideal sheaf data $I, J$: if for every $x \in X$ there is some affine open $U$ containing $x$ for which the germ ideals of $I$ and $J$ at $x$ computed on $U$ agree, then $I = J$. Third, for every $I$, every affine open $U$ and every $x \in U$: if $x$ does not lie in the support of $I$ then the germ ideal at $x$ is the whole stalk $\mathcal{O}_{X,x}$, and if $x$ does lie in the support of $I$ then the germ ideal at $x$ is contained in the maximal ideal of the local ring $\mathcal{O}_{X,x}$.
--
--   This is the basic stalk-level dictionary for quasi-coherent ideal sheaves: the germ ideal $I_x \subseteq \mathcal{O}_{X,x}$ is well defined independently of the affine chart, ideal sheaves are determined by their germ ideals, and the support is precisely the locus where the germ ideal is proper. It is used to compare ideal sheaves point by point, in particular in the specialisation lemmas for germ ideals and in the analysis of the ideal cutting out a point on a two-chart model of the modular curve $X_1$.
